# gorget-js snag #15g (2026-08-10, HIGH severity) — `gg fmt` corrupts
# string literals containing `\xHH` when the leading backslash is
# escaped in source.
#
# RED signature:
#     "with \\xHH escape"   # source: 18 quoted chars, value 16 bytes
#         ↓ gg fmt
#     "with \xHH escape"    # re-emitted with backslash DROPPED
#     value: "with xHH escape"   # 15 bytes — backslash is GONE
#
# The formatter's string re-emitter fails to preserve `\\` when the
# following character is `x` (or presumably `u`). Interestingly,
# `\\x41` (backslash + hex digits) is not mangled — only `\\x` +
# non-hex triggers the loss. Discovered in a gorget-js test-name
# string; live in every corpus that uses `\\x`/`\\u` escapes.
#
# INTENDED behavior: fmt output preserves the original string VALUE
# byte-for-byte. Round-trip via re-parse + AST-equivalence check.
#
# Class: same as Snag #2 (fmt data-loss, comment detach). This is
# stricter — Snag #2 was trivia loss, this changes program semantics.
#
# ⚠ LOCAL-REPRO CAVEAT (Gorget HEAD 2026-08-10, R39 close): five
# escape-shape variants tried (bare `\\x` + non-hex, `\\x` + hex,
# `\\x` alone, in struct/vardecl/call/for-loop/vector contexts,
# `\\xHH \\xGG \\xII` in one string, prefix/suffix around `\\x`) —
# ALL round-tripped byte-preserving on this Gorget. Cannot minimize
# further without the gorget-js reporter's exact source. Kept as
# a documentation fixture pending a reproducing shape; the R40+
# fix work owes a re-probe with the reporter's tree.
String s = "with \\xHH escape"
void main():
    print(s)
    print(f"len={s.len()}")
